---
const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Do I need a Cursor subscription?",
    a: "The catalog reflects what your Cursor account can use. Cursor subscription limits, model eligibility, and usage accounting still apply. Model Ferry only makes those models selectable in connected clients."
  },
  {
    q: "Where is my Cursor credential stored?",
    a: "Model Ferry never receives or stores your Cursor password. The browser sign-in mints a 90-day API key that the Cursor SDK keeps in ~/.cursor/sdk/auth.json, readable only by your user. Model Ferry attempts renewal before expiry. Set CURSOR_API_KEY to skip the browser flow."
  },
  {
    q: "Is Model Ferry affiliated with these agents?",
    a: "No. Model Ferry is an independent open-source project built on Cursor's public Agent SDK. It is not affiliated with, sponsored, or endorsed by Cursor, OpenCode, Pi, or Hermes. Those names and trademarks belong to their respective owners."
  },
  {
    q: "Which platforms does it support?",
    a: "macOS, Linux, and Windows 10/11. Use install.sh on macOS and Linux (launchd or systemd). Use install.ps1 on Windows (Task Scheduler). Manual npm setup works on all three."
  },
  {
    q: "Which agents can I use?",
    a: "OpenCode has the richest integration, including native Cursor variants. Setup also configures Pi and Hermes Agent when installed. Any other same-machine client that accepts a custom OpenAI Chat Completions provider can connect manually. Run modelferry agents to see recognized harnesses on your machine."
  },
  {
    q: "Does detecting an agent mean it is automatically compatible?",
    a: "No. Discovery and provider compatibility are separate. Model Ferry recognizes more than 40 harnesses so setup can explain what is installed, but it edits only verified config formats. Detected-only agents must support a custom OpenAI provider to connect manually."
  },
  {
    q: "Is anything sent off my machine?",
    a: "Prompts and model-visible tool context or results go to Cursor's servers (Anysphere) for inference. The SDK client and agent loop run locally. The bridge has no telemetry, binds to 127.0.0.1, and uses a per-install bearer token. Cursor agents are limited to MCP, so shell and file execution stay with your local client."
  },
  {
    q: "How does local auth work?",
    a: "Setup mints a random bearer token into ~/.config/modelferry/config.json and writes it into each configured client's Model Ferry provider. Chat, models, catalog refresh, and setup-page auth or config changes all require that token."
  },
  {
    q: "What if the default port is taken?",
    a: "The bridge scans the next 19 ports, binds the first free one, and records the resolved port so automatically configured providers, the onboarding page, and CLI all agree. Manually configured clients must use the resolved port shown by modelferry status."
  }
];
---
<section class="section" id="faq">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <span class="kicker">FAQ</span>
    <h2 class="heading">Frequently asked questions.</h2>
    <div class="list">
      {faqs.map((item) => (
        <details class="item">
          <summary>{item.q}</summary>
          <p>{item.a}</p>
        </details>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .container.narrow { max-width: 720px; }
  .heading { font-size: clamp(30px, 4vw, 44px); margin-bottom: 32px; }
  .item { border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .item:first-child { border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .item summary { list-style: none; cursor: pointer; padding: 20px 0; font-weight: 600; font-size: 16px; display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; }
  .item summary::-webkit-details-marker { display: none; }
  .item summary::after { content: "+"; color: var(--faint); font-size: 20px; font-weight: 400; margin-left: 16px; }
  .item[open] summary::after { content: "–"; }
  .item p { margin: 0 0 20px; color: var(--muted); font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7; max-width: 620px; }
</style>
